tel 1.0

base cacophony
import soundness

info   audio as typed, immutable PCM values: read and write WAV, AIFF, AIFC, AU and SND; record from and play to devices
detail
    _Cacophony_ reads and writes audio as typed values, records from the machine's inputs,
    and plays through its outputs. Reading a WAV, AIFF, AIFC, AU or SND file yields an
    `Audio` — an immutable block of PCM samples which knows its sample rate (a genuine
    quantity in hertz), its channel count, its bit depth and every sample it holds — and
    the file format lives in the type: an `Audio in Wave` is distinct from an `Audio in
    Aiff`, converting between them is `to[Aiff]`, and an audio value's encoded bytes are
    read from it as `Data`, with the same `read` as any other source.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    // A complete WAV file: one channel, 16-bit signed samples at 8 kHz, four frames.
    val wav = hex"""524946462c00000057415645666d74201000000001000100401f0000803e00000200100064
                    617461080000006400c8002c019001"""

    val audio: Audio in Wave = wav.read[Audio in Wave]
    audio.channels        // 1
    audio.frames          // 4
    audio.bitsPerSample   // 16
    audio.sampleRate      // 8000.0*Hertz
    audio.duration        // 0.0005*Second
    audio(0, 2)           // 300

    val aiff: Data = audio.to[Aiff].read[Data]
    aiff.read[Audio in Aiff](0, 2)   // 300
    ```

    Underneath, uncompressed audio is a grid of numbers played at a fixed rate, and the
    formats differ in their headers more than their substance; Java's sound library exposes
    it through mutable objects and untyped formats, where a wrong channel count surfaces as
    noise. Here the format is an `Audible` typeclass instance (`Wave`, `Aiff`, `Aifc`, `Au`,
    `Snd`), the channel layout an optional `ChannelLayout` carried in the type as `across
    Stereo` or `across Surround[6]`, and audio which is not already PCM is converted to
    16-bit signed PCM as it is read, so every `Audio` has the same internal form.

    Watching one audio value move through the pipeline shows how the pieces connect:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label Bytes become an audio value when the format is named
      code
          val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]
    frame
      label Re-expressed in another format, the samples are unchanged
      code
          val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]
          val converted = audio.to[Aiff]
          converted.frames   // 4
    frame
      label An audio value reads as its encoded bytes
      code
          val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]
          val converted = audio.to[Aiff]
          val bytes: Data = converted.read[Data]
    frame
      label And the bytes read back as audio in that format
      code
          val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]
          val converted = audio.to[Aiff]
          val bytes: Data = converted.read[Data]
          bytes.read[Audio in Aiff](0, 2)   // 300
    ##
    ```

    Devices are `Audio.Feed`s (inputs, listed by `Audio.Feed.list`) and `Audio.Outlet`s (outputs, listed by
    `Audio.Outlet.list`), each a `Audio.Device` with a name, vendor, description and the
    `configurations` it offers. `Audio.Feed#record` and `Audio.Outlet#play` hold a device for as long
    as the program wants it, returning a `Recording` or a `Playback`; for a device held for
    exactly one block, a line is *opened* as a file is, with the `Pcm` form —
    `feed.open[Pcm across Stereo](Read, PcmFlag.Rate(48000))` and `outlet.open[Pcm](Write)`
    — and released when the block ends, with playback gated on the `Write` grant so that
    the compiler, not the driver, refuses to play to a capture line.

    Reading raises `Audio.Error` through the `Tactic` in scope; recording raises
    `Audio.Feed.Error` and playback `Audio.Outlet.Error`. The library is one source set,
    `cacophony.core`, for the JVM, over `javax.sound.sampled`; on macOS the JVM must have
    been granted microphone permission or no feeds are listed. Everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella except `Audio.Device`, the shared parent of `Audio.Feed` and `Audio.Outlet`, which is
    reached as `cacophony.Device`.

refer cacophony.Audio
refer cacophony.Audible
refer cacophony.Audio.Feed
refer cacophony.Audio.Outlet
refer cacophony.Pcm

entry .Audio   an immutable block of PCM samples which knows its rate, channels, depth and every sample
  detail
      An `Audio` is pure and immutable: its samples are a frozen array, so a value may be
      shared freely. Its `Form` type member is the file format it was read in or converted
      to (`Audio in Wave`), and its `Domain` the channel layout, if one is known (`Audio
      across Stereo`); a plain `Audio` has neither. It is made by reading bytes —
      `data.read[Audio in Wave]` through `.aggregable`, or `data.read[Audio]` through
      `.aggregable2` when the format is to be detected — or built from samples with
      `Audio[Wave](format, samples)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val wav = hex"""524946462c00000057415645666d74201000000001000100401f0000803e00000200100064
                      617461080000006400c8002c019001"""

      val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]
      audio.sampleRate   // 8000.0*Hertz
      audio.duration     // 0.0005*Second
      audio(0, 0)        // 100
      audio.to[Au].read[Data].read[Audio in Au].frames   // 4
      ```

      Audio which is not already signed or unsigned PCM (µ-law in an AU file, say) is
      converted to 16-bit signed PCM as it is read, so `#apply` always indexes plain
      samples. An `Audio in form` is `Streamable` (`.streamable`), so it reads as `Data` and
      writes to a file handle directly, and `Abstractable` to an HTTP response body with
      the format's media type (`.abstractable`). `#to` re-expresses it in another format.
  refer cacophony.Audible
  refer cacophony.Wave
  refer cacophony.ChannelLayout
  entry .apply   reads audio from any byte source with the format detected, or builds audio from a format and samples
      `Audio(source)`, for any `Streamable by Data over Credit` source, lets
      `javax.sound.sampled` detect the file format, converting to 16-bit signed PCM if the
      encoding is not PCM, and returns a plain `Audio` with no `Form`; it raises
      `Audio.Error(Unset)` if the bytes are not audio in any format the JVM knows.

      `Audio[form](format: javax.sound.sampled.AudioFormat, data: Array[Byte]^{})` builds an
      `Audio in form` from raw PCM samples and the Java format which describes them, for
      audio generated in the program. The samples must be a frozen array: `Array.freeze` an
      exclusive one, or build them with `Array.tabulate`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import javax.sound.sampled as jss

      // One second of a 440 Hz sine: 16-bit signed little-endian, mono, 8 kHz.
      val rate = 8000
      val format = jss.AudioFormat(jss.AudioFormat.Encoding.PCM_SIGNED, rate, 16, 1, 2, rate, false)

      val samples: Data = Array.tabulate[Byte](2*rate): index =>
        val value = (Math.sin(2*Math.PI*440*(index/2)/rate)*32767).toInt
        (if index%2 == 0 then value else value >> 8).toByte

      val tone: Audio in Wave = Audio[Wave](format, samples)
      tone.duration   // 1.0*Second
      tone.read[Data].length   // the bytes of a WAV file
      ```
  entry .streamable   an `Audio in form` reads as its encoded bytes, and so writes to any destination
      The `Streamable by Data over Credit` given for any `Audio` whose `Form` has an
      `Audible`: `audio.read[Data]` is the complete file, headers and all, and
      `handle.write(audio)` writes it. The encoding is done by the JVM's writer for the
      format's name, so a format the JVM cannot write throws rather than raises.
  entry .streamableAcross   as `.streamable`, for an `Audio in form across layout`
      A separate given because the refined type with both members is not matched by the
      one with `Form` alone.
  entry .abstractable   an `Audio in form` as an HTTP response body, with its format's media type
      `audio/x-wav`, `audio/x-aiff`, `audio/x-aifc` or `audio/basic`, and the encoded bytes
      as the body, so an audio value can be served directly.
  entry .aggregable   `data.read[Audio in form]`: bytes read as audio in a named format
      The `Aggregable by Data` given for `Audio in form`, for any `form` with an `Audible`.
      Needs a `Tactic[Audio.Error]`, raised with the format if the bytes are not audio of
      that type: reading a WAV as `Audio in Aiff` fails, since the format is a claim about
      the bytes, not a request to convert them.
  entry .aggregable2   `data.read[Audio]`: bytes read as audio with the format detected
      The `Aggregable by Data` given for a plain `Audio`, through `Audio.apply`; the error
      names no format.
  entry .Error   raised when bytes are not audio in the expected format
    detail
        _unable to read the audio in `format` format_, with `unspecified` when the format was
        to be detected. Raised by `.aggregable` and `Audible#read` when the JVM cannot parse
        the bytes, when it parses them as a different format from the one named, or when it
        cannot convert their encoding to PCM.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        val broken = hex"5249464600000000ffffffff"
        capture[Audio.Error](broken.read[Audio in Wave])   // Audio.Error(Wave())
        ```
    entry #audible   the format expected, or `Unset` if the format was to be detected
  entry #channels   the number of channels: 1 for mono, 2 for stereo
  entry #bitsPerSample   the bit depth of each sample: 8, 16, 24 or 32
  entry #frames   the number of frames, each holding one sample per channel
  entry #sampleRate   the sample rate as a frequency: `8000.0*Hertz`
      A `Quantity[Seconds[-1]]`, so it participates in arithmetic with other quantities
      rather than being a bare number.
  entry #duration   the length of the audio in seconds: frames divided by the sample rate
      A `Quantity[Seconds[1]]`.
  entry #apply   the sample at a channel and frame, as a signed integer
      `audio(channel, frame)` decodes one sample from the bytes, honouring the format's
      byte order and signedness, and sign-extends it, so a 16-bit sample is in −32768 to
      32767. The indices are not checked against `#channels` and `#frames`.
  entry #to   the same audio re-expressed in another format: `audio.to[Aiff]`
      The samples are shared, not copied, and the channel layout is kept; only the `Form`
      changes, and with it the bytes the value reads as and the media type it serves with.

  entry .Configuration   a format a device's lines offer: channels, rate, bit depth, encoding and byte order
    detail
        Returned by `Device#configurations`, one per format the mixer's lines report. The
        sample rate is `Unset` when the line accepts any rate (the JVM reports −1), which is
        common.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Audio.Feed.list.map(_.configurations)
        ```
    refer cacophony.Audio.Device
    entry #channels   the number of channels the format has
    entry #sampleRate   the sample rate, or `Unset` if the line accepts any rate
    entry #bitsPerSample   the bit depth: 8, 16, 24 or 32
    entry #encoding   `Sonation.PcmSigned` or `Sonation.PcmUnsigned`
    entry #bigEndian   whether multi-byte samples are big-endian
  entry .Device   what a `Feed` and an `Outlet` share: a mixer the system describes, and the lines it offers
    detail
        A `Device` wraps a `javax.sound.sampled.Mixer.Info`, giving its `#name`, `#vendor` and
        `#description`, the `#configurations` its lines of the right kind (target lines for a
        `Feed`, source lines for an `Outlet`) report, and `#supports` to ask about one before
        committing to it. It is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella: a program names
        `Feed` or `Outlet`, or `cacophony.Device` for code over both.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Audio.Feed.list.map(feed => t"${feed.name} (${feed.vendor}): ${feed.description}")
        Audio.Outlet.list.head.let(_.supports[Stereo](44100.0*Hertz, 16))
        ```
    refer cacophony.Audio.Feed
    refer cacophony.Audio.Outlet
    entry #name   the device's name, as the system reports it
    entry #vendor   the device's vendor
    entry #description   the device's description
    entry #configurations   every format the device's lines report, as `Configuration`s
    entry #supports   whether the device can open a line in a layout at a rate and bit depth, as signed little-endian PCM
        `device.supports[Stereo](44100.0*Hertz, 16)` asks the mixer whether such a line is
        supported, without opening one; `record` and `play` raise `UnsupportedConfiguration`
        where this is `false`.
  entry .Feed   an input device: records audio in a chosen layout, rate and bit depth
    detail
        The machine's feeds are `Feed.list`; a machine may have none, so the first is an
        `Optional`. A feed is a `Device`, with a name, vendor, description and
        `configurations`, and `supports` to ask about a format before recording. `#record`
        opens a line and returns a `Recording` whose `stream` yields `Audio` chunks until it
        is stopped; for a line held for exactly one block, `feed.open[Pcm across Stereo](Read,
        PcmFlag.Rate(48000))` lends a `Pcm.PcmInput` instead (see `Pcm`).

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Audio.Feed.list.head.let: feed =>
          if feed.supports[Stereo](44100.0*Hertz, 16) then
            val recording = feed.record[Stereo](44100.0*Hertz, bits = 16)
            val firstChunk: Optional[Audio across Stereo] = recording.stream.head
            recording.stop()
        ```

        Recording from a feed which is unavailable or cannot honour the configuration raises
        `Feed.Error`. On macOS the JVM must have been granted microphone permission, or
        `Feed.list` is empty.
    refer cacophony.Recording
    refer cacophony.Pcm
    refer cacophony.Audio.Device
    entry .list   every mixer with a line to record from, as a `Feed`
        Enumerates the JVM's mixers and keeps those offering a `TargetDataLine`. The list is
        fresh on each call, so a device plugged in since is seen.
    entry .Error   raised when a feed cannot record
      detail
          Error 440: _could not record from feed `name` because …_, with the reason's number
          and message appended. Raised by `Feed#record` and by opening a feed as `Pcm`.
      entry .Reason   `Unavailable`, `UnsupportedConfiguration` or `Closed`
          `Unavailable` (1), _the audio line could not be opened_: another program holds it, or
          the system refused; `UnsupportedConfiguration` (2), _the requested configuration is
          not supported_, which `Device#supports` predicts; and `Closed` (3), _the recording
          has already been stopped_.
      entry #feed   the feed's name
      entry #reason   why it could not record
    entry #record   opens a line and records in a layout at a rate and bit depth, as a `Recording` of chunks
        `feed.record[Stereo](44100.0*Hertz, 16, chunkBytes = 65536)`: a line is opened for
        signed little-endian PCM in the layout's channels and started; the `Recording`'s
        `stream` reads chunks of up to `chunkBytes` from it, each an `Audio across layout`,
        until `stop` closes the line. Raises `Feed.Error` with `UnsupportedConfiguration` or
        `Unavailable`. The line is held until `stop` is called.
  entry .Outlet   an output device: plays an audio value in the background, or synchronously within a scope
    detail
        The machine's outlets are `Outlet.list`. An outlet is a `Device`, with a name, vendor,
        description, `configurations` and `supports`. `#play` opens a line for the audio's
        own format and returns a `Playback` which runs on its own thread, to be awaited or
        stopped; for a line held for exactly one block, `outlet.open[Pcm](Write)` lends a
        `Pcm.PcmOutput` whose `play` is synchronous and needs the `Write` grant (see `Pcm`).

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val wav = hex"""524946462c00000057415645666d74201000000001000100401f0000803e00000200100064
                        617461080000006400c8002c019001"""
        val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]

        Audio.Outlet.list.head.let: outlet =>
          val playback = outlet.play(audio)
          playback.await()

        Audio.Outlet.list.head.let: outlet =>
          outlet.open[Pcm](Write): out ?=>
            out.play(audio)
        ```

        Playing to an outlet which is unavailable or cannot accept the audio's configuration
        raises `Outlet.Error`.
    refer cacophony.Playback
    refer cacophony.Pcm
    refer cacophony.Audio.Device
    entry .list   every mixer with a line to play to, as an `Outlet`
        Enumerates the JVM's mixers and keeps those offering a `SourceDataLine`.
    entry .Error   raised when an outlet cannot play
      detail
          Error 374: _could not play to outlet `name` because …_, with the reason's number and
          message appended. Raised by `Outlet#play` and by `Pcm.play` on an opened outlet.
      entry .Reason   `Unavailable`, `UnsupportedConfiguration` or `Closed`
          `Unavailable` (1), _the audio line could not be opened_; `UnsupportedConfiguration`
          (2), _the requested configuration is not supported_: the audio's rate, depth or
          channels are not something the outlet can play, which `Device#supports` predicts;
          and `Closed` (3), _the playback has already been stopped_.
      entry #outlet   the outlet's name
      entry #reason   why it could not play
    entry #play   plays an audio value on a virtual thread, as a `Playback` to await or stop
        `outlet.play(audio, chunkBytes = 65536)` opens a line for the audio's format
        (`UnsupportedConfiguration` or `Unavailable` otherwise), starts it, and writes the
        samples in chunks from a background thread, draining the line and closing it when
        the audio ends. `Playback#stop` is observed between chunks and closes the line at
        once; `await` joins the thread. Any `Audio` plays, whatever its `Form`: the samples
        are PCM already.

entry .Audible   a file format which audio can be read in and written in: its name and media type
  detail
      The typeclass behind `Audio in form`: `Wave`, `Aiff`, `Aifc`, `Au` and `Snd` each
      have an instance in their companion. `#name` is the type name `javax.sound.sampled`
      knows the format by (`WAVE`, `AIFF`, `AIFC`, `AU`, `SND`), which must match what the
      JVM detects for a read to succeed, and `#mediaType` is what an audio value is served
      as. `#read` is the reading itself, which `Audio.aggregable` delegates to.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      summon[Wave is Audible].name        // t"WAVE"
      summon[Aiff is Audible].mediaType   // media"audio/x-aiff"
      Wave().name                         // t"WAVE"
      ```
  refer cacophony.Audio
  refer cacophony.Wave
  entry #name   the format's type name as the JVM's audio system reports it
  entry #mediaType   the format's media type, for serving audio over HTTP
  entry #read   reads a byte source as `Audio in Self`, raising `Audio.Error` if it is not this format
      Parses the file format, checks it is this one, converts a non-PCM encoding to 16-bit
      signed PCM, and reads every sample into memory. Needs a `Tactic[Audio.Error]`.

entry .Wave   the WAV (RIFF) format, as `Audio in Wave`
    A sealed type with no values, used as a type argument; its companion holds the
    `Audible` instance, `audibility`, with the name `WAVE` and the media type `audio/x-wav`.
    `Wave()` returns that instance as an `Audible`, which is how `Audio.Error` names a
    format.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val wav = hex"""524946462c00000057415645666d74201000000001000100401f0000803e00000200100064
                    617461080000006400c8002c019001"""
    wav.read[Audio in Wave].frames   // 4
    ```

entry .Aiff   the AIFF format, as `Audio in Aiff`
    A sealed type whose companion holds the `Audible` instance, `audibility`, with the name
    `AIFF` and the media type `audio/x-aiff`; `Aiff()` returns it. An AIFF file is
    big-endian, which `Audio#apply` honours when indexing a sample.

entry .Aifc   the AIFF-C format, as `Audio in Aifc`
    A sealed type whose companion holds the `Audible` instance, `audibility`, with the name
    `AIFC` and the media type `audio/x-aifc`; `Aifc()` returns it.

entry .Au   Sun's AU format, as `Audio in Au`
    A sealed type whose companion holds the `Audible` instance, `audibility`, with the name
    `AU` and the media type `audio/basic`; `Au()` returns it. An AU file holding µ-law or
    A-law audio is converted to 16-bit signed PCM as it is read.

entry .Snd   the NeXT SND format, as `Audio in Snd`
    A sealed type whose companion holds the `Audible` instance, `audibility`, with the name
    `SND` and the media type `audio/basic`; `Snd()` returns it.

entry .ChannelLayout   a channel layout carried in the type: how many channels, known statically
  detail
      The typeclass behind `Audio across layout`, `Recording across layout` and
      `Pcm across layout`: `Monaural` has one channel, `Stereo` two, and `Surround[n]` the
      literal `n`. A feed records in a layout — `feed.record[Stereo](…)` — so the channel
      count of what it produces is in the type, not a number to be checked.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      summon[Monaural is ChannelLayout].channels      // 1
      summon[Stereo is ChannelLayout].channels        // 2
      summon[Surround[6] is ChannelLayout].channels   // 6
      ```
  refer cacophony.Stereo
  refer cacophony.Surround
  entry #channels   the number of channels in the layout

entry .Monaural   the one-channel layout, as `Audio across Monaural`
    A sealed type whose companion holds its `ChannelLayout` instance, `channelLayout`, with
    one channel.

entry .Stereo   the two-channel layout, as `Audio across Stereo`
    A sealed type whose companion holds its `ChannelLayout` instance, `channelLayout`, with
    two channels.

entry .Surround   a surround layout of a literal number of channels, as `Audio across Surround[6]`
    `Surround[N]` takes a literal `Int`, and its companion's `channelLayout` given yields
    that number of channels for any `N` with a `ValueOf`, so `Surround[6]` and
    `Surround[8]` are distinct layouts with no instance written for either.

entry .Sonation   how samples are encoded: `PcmSigned` or `PcmUnsigned`
    The encoding a device `Audio.Configuration` reports. Every `Audio` value is signed PCM after
    reading, so `PcmUnsigned` appears only in what a device says it supports.

entry .Recording   an open recording: a stream of audio chunks until it is stopped
  detail
      Returned by `Audio.Feed#record`. `#stream` is a lazy chain of `Audio across Domain` chunks,
      each up to the chunk size the recording was started with, read from the line as the
      chain is forced; it ends when `#stop` closes the line, or when the line delivers
      nothing. The `Domain` type member is the layout recorded in.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Audio.Feed.list.head.let: feed =>
        val recording = feed.record[Monaural](8000.0*Hertz, 16, chunkBytes = 1024)
        val chunk: Optional[Audio across Monaural] = recording.stream.head
        recording.stop()
        chunk.let(_.channels)   // 1
      ```
  refer cacophony.Audio.Feed
  entry #active   whether the recording has not yet been stopped
  entry #stop   stops the line and closes it; the stream ends
      Idempotent. The line is held until this is called, so a recording should always be
      stopped; `Audio.Feed`'s scoped form, `open[Pcm across layout]`, does it at the end of the
      block.
  entry #stream   the chunks recorded so far and to come, as a lazy chain
      Each force reads one chunk from the line, blocking until it is full, so the chain is
      consumed at the rate the device produces samples.

entry .Playback   audio playing in the background: await its end, or stop it early
  detail
      Returned by `Audio.Outlet#play`. The samples are written from a virtual thread; `#await`
      blocks until the audio has been played to the end (or stopped), and `#stop` ends it
      early, flushing what has not yet sounded. Whichever of `stop` and the end of the
      audio comes first closes the line, exactly once.
  refer cacophony.Audio.Outlet
  entry #active   whether the audio is still playing
  entry #stop   stops the playback at once, discarding what has not yet sounded, and closes the line
      Idempotent, and safe to call from another thread than the one which started the
      playback: the writer observes it between chunks.
  entry #await   blocks until the playback has finished, by reaching the end or being stopped

entry .Pcm   the form for scoped audio-line access: a feed opens as `Pcm across layout`, an outlet as `Pcm`
  detail
      Where `Audio.Feed#record` and `Audio.Outlet#play` hold a device for as long as the program wants
      it, a line may instead be *opened* for exactly one block, as a file is: the OS line
      lasts precisely as long as the scope and is released however it ends. `Pcm` is the
      form named at the `open`: a `Audio.Feed` opens as `Pcm across layout` for capture, lending
      a `.PcmInput` whose `stream` yields `Audio across layout` chunks; an `Audio.Outlet` opens as
      plain `Pcm` for playback, lending a `.PcmOutput` whose `.play` plays each `Audio`
      synchronously. The `Openable` instances, `.feedOpenable` and `.outletOpenable`, are
      in the companion, so no import is needed.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val wav = hex"""524946462c00000057415645666d74201000000001000100401f0000803e00000200100064
                      617461080000006400c8002c019001"""
      val audio = wav.read[Audio in Wave]

      Audio.Feed.list.head.let: feed =>
        feed.open[Pcm across Stereo](Read, PcmFlag.Rate(48000), PcmFlag.Chunk(1024)): input ?=>
          input.stream.head

      Audio.Outlet.list.head.let: outlet =>
        outlet.open[Pcm](Write): out ?=>
          out.play(audio)
      ```

      The configuration is given as `PcmFlag`s: `PcmFlag.Rate(hertz)` (44100 by default),
      `PcmFlag.Bits(bits)` (16) and `PcmFlag.Chunk(bytes)` (65536); an outlet reads only
      `Chunk`, since each `play` opens a line for the audio's own format. Playing requires
      the `Write` grant: `outlet.open[Pcm]()` with no grant lends a `PcmOutput` on which
      `play` does not compile, so a line opened for capture cannot be played to, and the
      compiler, not the audio driver, says so.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val outlet: Audio.Outlet = ???
      val audio: Audio = ???

      outlet.open[Pcm](): out ?=>
        out.play(audio)   // does not compile
      ```
  refer cacophony.Audio.Feed
  refer cacophony.Audio.Outlet
  refer cacophony.PcmFlag
  entry .PcmInput   the handle lent by opening a `Feed` as `Pcm across layout`: a stream of audio chunks
    detail
        An exclusive capability over an open, started line, valid for the block it is lent
        to. `#stream` reads chunks as it is forced, each an `Audio across layout` of up to
        the `PcmFlag.Chunk` size; the line is stopped and closed when the block ends.
    entry #stream   the chunks captured, as a lazy chain, for as long as the scope lasts
  entry .PcmOutput   the handle lent by opening an `Outlet` as `Pcm`: plays audio values, with the `Write` grant
    detail
        An exclusive capability over the outlet, valid for the block it is lent to. Each
        `Pcm.play` (the grant-gated extension) or `#playAudio` opens a line for the audio's
        own format, plays it to completion and closes it; the next may have a different
        format.
    entry #playAudio   opens a line for the audio's format, plays it to the end, and closes the line
        Public so that the grant-gated `play` extension can reach it; use `play`, which
        checks the `Write` grant, rather than this. Raises `Audio.Outlet.Error` with
        `UnsupportedConfiguration` or `Unavailable`.
  entry .play   `out.play(audio)` on a `PcmOutput` opened with the `Write` grant: synchronous playback
      An extension on `PcmOutput & Granting[Grant.Write]`, so it is available only inside
      `outlet.open[Pcm](Write)`. Blocks until the audio has sounded, and raises
      `Audio.Outlet.Error` if the outlet cannot play it.
  entry .FeedOpenable   the `Openable` which opens a `Feed` as `Pcm across layout`, lending a `PcmInput`
    detail
        A named class rather than an anonymous given, so that its type members are stable:
        `Self = Audio.Feed`, `Form = Pcm across layout`, `Operand = PcmFlag`, `Result =
        PcmInput[layout]`. Made by `.feedOpenable` from the `ChannelLayout` and the
        `Tactic[Audio.Feed.Error]` in scope.
    entry #open   opens a line for the flags' rate, bits and chunk size in the layout's channels, for the block
        Signed little-endian PCM at `PcmFlag.Rate` (44100 by default) and `PcmFlag.Bits`
        (16); the line is started before the block and stopped and closed after it. Raises
        `Audio.Feed.Error` with `UnsupportedConfiguration` or `Unavailable`.
  entry .OutletOpenable   the `Openable` which opens an `Outlet` as `Pcm`, lending a `PcmOutput`
    detail
        `Self = Audio.Outlet`, `Form = Pcm`, `Operand = PcmFlag`, `Result = PcmOutput`. Made by
        `.outletOpenable` from the `Tactic[Audio.Outlet.Error]` in scope. Opening itself touches
        no line: each `play` within the block opens one for its audio.
    entry #open   lends a `PcmOutput` with the flags' chunk size to the block
  entry .feedOpenable   the given which makes `feed.open[Pcm across layout](…)` available
      Needs a `ChannelLayout` for the layout and a `Tactic[Audio.Feed.Error]`, which it captures.
      In implicit scope through the companion.
  entry .outletOpenable   the given which makes `outlet.open[Pcm](…)` available
      Needs a `Tactic[Audio.Outlet.Error]`, which it captures. In implicit scope through the
      companion.

entry .PcmFlag   the options of a scoped line: `Rate(hertz)`, `Bits(bits)` and `Chunk(bytes)`
    Passed to `open[Pcm …]` after the mode. `Rate(hertz)` is the sample rate in hertz
    (44100 if absent), `Bits(bits)` the bit depth (16), and `Chunk(bytes)` the size of each
    chunk read from a feed or written to an outlet (65536). A feed reads all three; an
    outlet reads only `Chunk`, since its lines take the format of the audio played.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    Audio.Feed.list.head.let: feed =>
      feed.open[Pcm across Monaural](Read, PcmFlag.Rate(8000), PcmFlag.Bits(16), PcmFlag.Chunk(1024)):
        input ?=> input.stream.head.let(_.channels)   // 1
    ```
